 "narrative": "Estimates are already signed to the claimed direction, so positive values support the claim and negative values oppose it. H13 asks whether KEAP1 loss, compared with KEAP1 intact samples, is associated with a less negative composite dependency score, i.e., weaker composite dependency. The discovery contrast is +0.832 [0.766, 0.897] and the independent validation contrast is +0.795 [0.702, 0.889]. Both lower bounds are far above the 0.15-unit threshold. This strongly supports H13 as a within-dataset association: KEAP1-loss samples have markedly weaker composite dependency. I accept H13 and close it. This should not be interpreted as a causal KEAP1-knockout dependency or a claim about external generalizability; the endpoint is a composite score and the observed contrast may reflect the broader genomic/cell-state context of KEAP1 loss.\n\nH57 tested PTEN loss in a genetically cleaner no-coalteration background (TP53, SMARCA4, and KEAP1 intact). The discovery contrast was +0.252 [0.169, 0.335], which supports stronger composite dependency. The newly delivered validation contrast was +0.186 [0.066, 0.307]. Its point estimate is above the threshold and its direction is concordant, but the independent interval lower bound is below 0.15 units, so the validation confirms direction less decisively than it does for the top-tier SMARCA4, TP53, or KEAP1 effects. I therefore accept H57 as a dataset-specific PTEN-loss stronger-dependency association in that clean context, but with moderate rather than high confidence. The investigation remains active because the PTEN effect is moderate, was discovered after context refinement, and may be sensitive to co-occurring alterations or broader population contexts. The exact H57 claim can be treated as supported, while broader PTEN claims remain unresolved.\n\nOverall, the strongest supported findings in this supplied dataset are associations, not causal effects: SMARCA4 loss is associated with stronger composite dependency, while TP53 loss and KEAP1 loss are associated with weaker composite dependency overall, with independent validation. SMARCA4 and KEAP1 associations also remain substantial in several genetic and library contexts, and TP53 is robust in SMARCA4-intact backgrounds. PTEN loss now has a supported but less certain stronger-dependency association in a TP53/SMARCA4/KEAP1-intact context. Negative findings remain informative: KRAS mutation, MTAP loss, ARID1A loss, STK11 loss, MYC amplification, BRCA2 loss, CDKN2A loss, and research_marker_d_positive did not support the claimed meaningful effects in the tested directions. Several attenuation interactions are rejected, including SMARCA4 by TP53, KEAP1 by SMARCA4, SMARCA4 by high serum, TP53 by high serum, SMARCA4 by KEAP1, and the clean Avana SMARCA4-intact KEAP1-by-TP53 interaction. Global technical and culture probes for Avana library, KY library, matched omics, and media 10% versus 5% are below the 0.15-unit threshold and do not indicate large global confounding for these comparisons.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty remains around three issues. First, PTEN overall and in other co-alteration/subtype contexts is still not fully characterized: H9 direct evidence is borderline, H57 is context-specific, and subtype and co-alteration contexts are incomplete. Second, apparent KEAP1-by-TP53 context dependence is inconsistent: the overall reversed interaction H53 is suggestive, adenocarcinoma H55 is suggestive, but the cleaner Avana/SMARCA4-intact H54 is near zero. Third, high-serum attenuation of KEAP1-loss remains suggestive but fragile: H48 and H62 are below the effect-size lower-bound threshold, while cleaner background tests H56 and KY-library H59 do not support meaningful attenuation. Outside-Avana TP53 estimates and several subtype/age/marker probes also remain imprecise.\n\nThe most informative next analyses are therefore PTEN context analyses: whether the PTEN-loss stronger-dependency association persists among TP53-loss, SMARCA4-loss, and KEAP1-loss contexts; whether it remains in a broader TP53-intact/KEAP1-intact context that allows SMARCA4 loss; whether it is visible outside the Avana library; and whether it is present in adenocarcinoma within the clean no-coalteration background. A KEAP1-loss adult-subgroup probe adds a simple robustness check for the now-supported KEAP1 association. If PTEN remains meaningful only in the clean background, it should remain a context-specific finding; if it persists across several contexts and direct overall/subtype tests become stronger, it could be accepted more broadly. All conclusions remain associations within this supplied lung-lineage composite-score dataset and do not imply causality or external generalizability.",
 "research_notes": "Round 4 synthesis update. H13 KEAP1-loss weaker dependency is independently validated and closed. H57 PTEN-loss stronger dependency in TP53/SMARCA4/KEAP1-intact context is accepted but kept active due moderate size, validation lower bound below 0.15, and context-selected discovery. Top-tier supported associations: SMARCA4-loss stronger dependency; TP53-loss weaker dependency overall and SMARCA4-intact; KEAP1-loss weaker dependency overall; PTEN-loss stronger dependency in clean context with moderate confidence. Broad technical global confounding probes are below threshold. KEAP1-by-TP53 context dependence remains inconsistent and lower priority than PTEN: H53/H55 suggestive, H54 clean Avana/SMARCA4-intact rejects. High-serum KEAP1 attenuation remains inconsistent and lower priority: H48/H62 suggestive, H56/H59 do not support. Outside-Avana TP53, subtype/age, and marker_e/f remain unresolved. Next analyze should prioritize PTEN co-alteration contexts, PTEN non-Avana context, PTEN adenocarcinoma/clean-background, and adult KEAP1 robustness. Do not accept H9 overall PTEN solely from H57; require overall or broad context evidence. Interpret all claims as dataset-specific associations, not causal or generalizable claims."
